return [
    11 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Branding and Store Identity',
                'summary' => 'A store buyers remember and return to.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A marketplace store is a brand, not just a list of products. Sellers who build a clear identity earn more attention, more trust, and more repeat buyers than those who list without a story.\n\nStart with the store name and the promise. What does your store stand for, and what can a buyer always expect from it? A store that promises clean, graded, fast-delivered produce and keeps that promise becomes the store buyers search for by name.\n\nMake the identity visible. A consistent profile image, a clear store description, and a tidy layout signal professionalism. Buyers judge a store in seconds, and the first impression decides whether they browse or leave. The small effort of a complete profile pays for itself in trust.\n\nKeep the promise in every order. Branding is not the banner; it is the delivery of the same quality, the same packing standard, and the same response time, every time. The seller who is consistent builds a reputation that no single promotion can buy.\n\nFinally, let the identity grow with the business. As your range and volume grow, refine the store to match. The seller who treats the store as a brand, rather than a shelf, builds an asset that appreciates in value with every satisfied buyer.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Seasonal Selling and Demand Planning',
                'summary' => 'Sell the right product at the right time.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Demand on the marketplace is not flat; it moves with seasons, festivals, weather, and harvests. Seasonal selling and demand planning is the discipline of having the right product at the right time, and it is where the smart money is made.\n\nStart by reading the calendar. Which products sell more at which times of year, and which events drive demand in your region? The sales record from last year is the best forecast for this year, so keep it and study it before you plan your stock.\n\nPlan stock ahead of the peak. The sellers who profit from a festival or a season are the ones who stocked weeks before the demand arrived, not the ones who chased it. Order or produce early, because the cheap stock is the stock bought before everyone else wants it.\n\nManage the troughs too. Off-season demand is lower, and the sellers who survive it are those who planned for it: lighter stock, careful cash, and a focus on the products that sell year-round. The seller who only plans for the peak is the seller who drowns in the trough.\n\nFinally, watch the trend signals: search terms, inquiries, and what competitors run out of. The seller who reads the market early and positions before the crowd captures the season rather than chasing it.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Bulk and Wholesale Orders',
                'summary' => 'Serve buyers who order in volume.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The marketplace connects you to more than retail buyers. Bulk and wholesale orders, from traders, hotels, processors, and retailers, can become the backbone of a seller's business, but they demand different handling.\n\nStart with a wholesale offer. State your minimum order, your bulk pricing, and your lead time clearly, so a wholesale buyer knows whether you can serve them before they inquire. The buyer who can read your terms is the buyer who orders with confidence.\n\nConfirm capacity before you commit. A wholesale order you cannot fill is worse than an order you decline: it costs the buyer, your rating, and the relationship. Check your stock and your production or supply before you accept a large order, and be honest about the lead time.\n\nHandle the logistics deliberately. Bulk orders need packing that survives transport, a delivery plan that fits the buyer, and a payment arrangement through the platform. The wholesale sale that arrives intact, on time, and paid through the platform is the one that repeats.\n\nFinally, build the relationship. A reliable wholesale buyer is worth many retail ones. The seller who serves volume buyers with clear terms and reliable delivery builds a business with a steady floor of demand beneath the retail ups and downs.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Export and Distant Market Readiness',
                'summary' => 'Prepare to sell beyond your region.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Distant markets, including export, pay the best prices but demand the most preparation. Export and distant market readiness is the discipline of meeting grades, documentation, and logistics that local sales never require.\n\nStart with the grade. Distant buyers buy to a specification: size, quality, moisture, and packaging. Learn the grade your target market demands, and sort and pack to that grade, because a shipment that fails inspection is a loss that costs the seller the whole order, not just the margin.\n\nMaster the documentation. Distant sales require what local sales do not: grading certificates, phytosanitary documents, packing lists, and traceability records. The seller who keeps these ready, and can produce them on request, opens markets that the unprepared seller cannot enter.\n\nPlan the logistics and the cost. Freight, insurance, and delays are real costs that belong in the price. Quote the delivered price, not the farm-gate price, and know the route and the timeline so you can promise a delivery you can keep.\n\nFinally, start small and prove yourself. A first distant order delivered perfectly opens the door to the second, larger one. The seller who treats the first export order as a reputation, not just a sale, builds a business with far higher ceilings.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Seller Finance and Cash Flow',
                'summary' => 'Keep the business solvent while it grows.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Many profitable sellers fail on cash flow, not on profit. Seller finance and cash flow is the discipline of knowing when money comes in, when it goes out, and how to bridge the gap between them.\n\nStart with a cash forecast. List what you expect to receive and what you expect to pay, week by week, for the next season. The forecast reveals the squeeze before it arrives, and a squeeze seen early is a squeeze you can plan around.\n\nSeparate the flows. Keep business money apart from personal money, and reinvest a planned share of profit rather than spending it all. The seller who reinvests deliberately grows; the seller who spends the harvest each time starts every season from zero.\n\nManage the timing levers. Payment terms, stock levels, and order timing all move cash. Where the platform pays on delivery, the seller's cash arrives faster; where it pays on a cycle, the seller must plan the gap. Know your cycle, and price it into your offers.\n\nFinally, keep a reserve. A reserve covering a slow month or a failed delivery is what turns a setback into a delay rather than a collapse. The seller who manages cash, not just sales, survives the slow season and grows through it.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    12 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Using Data to Target Enrollment',
                'summary' => 'Find the growers the program needs.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Enrollment is not about signing everyone up; it is about reaching the growers the program is designed for, in the places they actually farm. Using data to target enrollment turns a scattered campaign into a precise one.\n\nStart with the map, not the guess. Where are the farms, and which ones are already in the registry? The map of existing registrations against the map of farming areas shows the gaps: the villages and blocks where eligible growers are not yet enrolled.\n\nPrioritise by program goals. If the program targets a crop, a region, or a scale of farm, rank the gaps by fit. A coordinator who enrolls with a target in mind fills the registry with the growers the program can actually serve, rather than with names that never convert.\n\nUse the field data to aim the agents. Send agents to the gap areas with the highest expected yield of eligible growers, and track their enrollment against the target. The coordinator who aims the field force with data covers more ground with the same people.\n\nFinally, measure and adjust weekly. Enrollment that is counted daily and reviewed weekly reveals what is working: which areas, which agents, which messages. The coordinator who reads the enrollment data turns recruitment from hope into management.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Field Force Communications',
                'summary' => 'Keep every agent informed and aligned.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A state's field force only performs if it is informed. Field force communications is the discipline of keeping every agent aligned on the standard, the current campaign, and the day's priorities, across a wide geography.\n\nStart with a simple, reliable channel. One agreed channel for the field force, used consistently, beats five channels used sporadically. Whether it is a messaging group, a daily briefing, or a weekly call, the channel must reach every agent and be checked by every agent.\n\nCommunicate the standard, not just the task. Agents who understand why a standard exists apply it in the field; agents who only receive a task list apply it loosely. Every message about a target or a campaign should carry the reason, because the reason is what survives contact with a difficult farm.\n\nMake communication two-way. The field sees what the office cannot: the blocked road, the new pest, the village that needs a visit. A coordinator who asks, and listens, turns the field force into the state's early-warning system, not just its hands.\n\nFinally, confirm understanding. A message not confirmed is a message not received. The coordinator who closes the loop, and hears back from every agent, runs a field force that moves together rather than a group of individuals guessing in different directions.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Coordinating with Providers and Buyers',
                'summary' => 'Align the supply chain around the grower.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The state coordinator sits between growers, providers, and buyers. Coordinating with providers and buyers is the discipline of aligning that supply chain so the grower's production actually reaches a market at a fair price.\n\nStart by knowing your providers and buyers. Which input providers serve the state, which service providers cover which areas, and which buyers are actively purchasing? A coordinator who knows the supply chain can connect a grower to the right provider or buyer in minutes, rather than leaving the grower to search.\n\nMatch supply to demand. When a harvest is coming, alert the buyers; when a buyer has a need, alert the growers. The coordinator who actively matches the two sides moves produce faster and at better prices, and the platform becomes the place the whole chain relies on.\n\nResolve the friction. The disputes, the delayed deliveries, and the failed verifications all land on the coordinator. Handling them fast and fairly keeps both sides on the platform, and a coordinator who resolves friction well keeps the supply chain flowing.\n\nFinally, measure the connections: growers linked to providers, produce matched to buyers, and disputes resolved on time. The coordinator who coordinates the chain, not just the registry, is the coordinator whose state actually thrives.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'State-Level Program Design',
                'summary' => 'Design programs the state can deliver.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "National programs succeed or fail at state level, and the difference is usually design. State-level program design is the discipline of turning a national goal into a program the state's people, budget, and geography can actually deliver.\n\nStart with the state's reality. What grows here, who farms it, what do they need, and what can the state's field force realistically cover? A program designed for a different geography, imported whole, fails; a program designed around the state's own facts succeeds.\n\nSet a few clear targets. A program with ten goals achieves none; one with two or three measurable targets achieves them. Choose the targets that matter most, define how they will be measured, and let the whole state see them.\n\nDesign the delivery before the launch. Who does what, with which agents, in which areas, by when? A program without a delivery plan is a press release; a program with a delivery plan is a set of tasks that agents can execute week by week.\n\nFinally, build in the review. Programs that work are programs that are adjusted: monthly review against the targets, and the courage to change what is not working. The coordinator who designs programs to be delivered and reviewed is the coordinator whose state actually progresses.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Crisis Management in the Field',
                'summary' => 'Lead when something goes wrong.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Every state faces the crisis: a pest outbreak, a flood, a failed delivery, a data breach, or a public dispute. Crisis management in the field is the discipline of leading through it with clear information and fast, honest action.\n\nStart with the facts, fast. In a crisis, the first need is to know what is happening, where, and how widely. Gather the facts from the field before deciding anything, because a decision made on rumour is a decision made wrong.\n\nSet the priority: people, then the platform, then the record. Protect the people affected, contain the damage to the program, and only then turn to the documentation. The coordinator who keeps the priorities straight in a crisis makes fewer mistakes under pressure.\n\nCommunicate honestly and quickly. Tell the field, the growers, and the office what is known, what is being done, and what is not yet known. Silence in a crisis breeds rumour, and rumour does more damage than the event itself.\n\nFinally, learn after it passes. Every crisis leaves a lesson: what failed, what worked, and what to prepare next time. The coordinator who records the lesson turns each crisis into the state's resilience, and leads the field force through the next one calmer than the last.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    13 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Building a Learning Organisation',
                'summary' => 'Make the whole organisation smarter.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A national operation is only as smart as its ability to learn. Building a learning organisation is the discipline of turning the experience of hundreds of people into knowledge the whole organisation can use.\n\nStart with the belief that the field knows most. The growers, agents, and coordinators closest to the work see what works and what fails. A learning organisation treats their experience as data, not as anecdote, and builds the channels to capture it.\n\nCreate the routines. A weekly field report, a monthly review, and a quarterly reflection turn scattered experience into a pattern. The learning organisation is not built by inspiration but by routines that make reflection unavoidable.\n\nShare what is learned, widely. A lesson discovered in one state and applied in all states is the whole value of a national operation. The coordinator who shares a fix across states multiplies it, and the organisation that shares learns exponentially.\n\nFinally, reward learning, not just results. When a team reports a failure honestly and learns from it, that honesty is more valuable than a lucky success. The organisation that rewards candour learns; the one that punishes it only repeats its mistakes, quietly, forever.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Technology and Data Platforms',
                'summary' => 'Choose and govern the platform well.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A national program runs on technology, and the choices about that technology shape everything the program can do. Technology and data platforms is the discipline of choosing, configuring, and governing the systems the operation depends on.\n\nStart with the requirements, not the tools. What must the system do: register growers, verify farms, move money, report impact? The requirements drive the choice of platform, and a platform chosen before the requirements are written is a solution looking for a problem.\n\nConfigure for the process, not the defaults. Every workflow, every field, and every role in the platform should mirror how the operation actually works. A poorly configured system makes the field force work around it, and every workaround is a source of error.\n\nGovern the data as an asset. Who owns each data set, who may change it, and who may see it are governance questions, not technical ones. A national platform with clear data ownership is trustworthy; one without it becomes a swamp of conflicting records.\n\nFinally, keep the human in the loop. Technology serves the program, not the reverse. The leader who governs the platform deliberately, and keeps it aligned to the mission, builds an operation that scales without losing its purpose.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Risk Management at National Scale',
                'summary' => 'See and defuse risks before they fire.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "At national scale, everything that can go wrong eventually does. Risk management at national scale is the discipline of seeing the risks early and defusing them before they become crises.\n\nStart with a risk register. List what could go wrong across the operation: fraud, data loss, a failed partner, a pest outbreak, a funding gap. A written register turns vague worry into specific, owned risks, each with an owner and a response.\n\nAssess by likelihood and impact. Not every risk deserves equal attention. The risks that are both likely and severe get the mitigation; the unlikely and minor get a note. The leader who triages risks spends effort where it protects the most.\n\nBuild the controls. Verification standards, audit trails, access controls, and backups are the controls that turn a risk into a managed one. Each control should map to a specific risk, because a control that guards nothing is cost without benefit.\n\nFinally, rehearse and review. A risk plan that is never tested is a plan that fails on the day. The organisation that rehearses its responses and reviews its register quarterly is the one that meets the inevitable crisis prepared, not surprised.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Partnerships and Alliances',
                'summary' => 'Grow the mission through partners.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "No national program is delivered alone. Partnerships and alliances is the discipline of choosing the right partners, structuring the relationship, and keeping it valuable to both sides over time.\n\nStart with the strategic fit. A partner should advance the mission, not merely decorate it. Ask what the partner brings that the program lacks, and what the program offers the partner, because a partnership that only benefits one side does not last.\n\nWrite the terms clearly. Roles, contributions, expectations, and how success is measured belong in an agreement, not in a handshake. The partnership with clear terms survives the first disagreement; the one without them does not.\n\nManage the relationship actively. A partner is not a vendor to be forgotten: regular communication, shared review, and honest feedback keep the alliance working. The program manager who tends the partnership harvests more value from it than the one who signs and moves on.\n\nFinally, measure what the partnership delivers. An alliance that cannot show its contribution is one that quietly consumes effort. The leader who tracks and reports the value of each partnership builds a network that compounds the mission's reach.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Strategy and Annual Planning',
                'summary' => 'Turn the mission into a year of work.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Strategy is the choice of what to do and, just as importantly, what not to do. Strategy and annual planning is the discipline of turning a mission into a year the organisation can actually execute.\n\nStart with the diagnosis. Where is the program now, what is working, what is failing, and what is changing around it? A plan built on a clear diagnosis is a plan aimed at reality; one built on hope is a wish.\n\nChoose the few priorities. An annual plan with five priorities has none. The organisations that progress choose two or three priorities, commit resources to them, and refuse the distractions that do not serve them.\n\nResource the plan honestly. Every priority needs people, budget, and time, and the plan must name where they come from. A priority without a resource is a hope, and the plan that over-commits collapses by the second quarter.\n\nFinally, set the review rhythm. A plan that is reviewed monthly and adjusted is a living tool; a plan filed in a drawer is a wasted afternoon. The leader who plans deliberately and reviews relentlessly is the leader whose strategy becomes results.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    14 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Platform Configuration Deep Dive',
                'summary' => 'Configure the platform with precision.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The platform's behaviour is set by its configuration, and a super admin who understands it deeply can shape the platform to the mission rather than fight it. Platform configuration deep dive is about mastering those levers.\n\nStart with the configuration model. What is a setting, what is a feature flag, and what is a structural change that needs a deployment? Knowing the boundary between a config change and a code change is what lets you move fast without breaking production.\n\nChange one thing at a time. In configuration, as in medicine, the first rule is to do no harm. Make one change, observe the result, and only then make the next, because a dozen changes made at once make the fault untraceable.\n\nKeep a change log. Every configuration change should be recorded: what changed, why, when, and who approved it. The log is the audit trail that answers the question every platform eventually faces: what changed, and why is the platform behaving differently now.\n\nFinally, test in a staging copy before production. A configuration change rehearsed in staging is a change that lands cleanly; one rehearsed only in production is a change that may take the platform down. The super admin who rehearses is the one who never has to apologise.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Data Protection and Privacy',
                'summary' => 'Handle personal data as a trust.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The platform holds personal data: names, phone numbers, locations, and financial details. Data protection and privacy is the discipline of handling that data as a trust, because a breach is a betrayal of every user.\n\nStart with the principle of least access. Every person and every role sees only the data their work requires, and no more. Access that is broader than the job is access that leaks, and the super admin who grants it owns the leak.\n\nCollect only what is needed. Every field on a form is a liability as well as an asset. The data you never collect is the data you can never lose, so each collected field must justify itself against the purpose it serves.\n\nProtect it technically and procedurally. Strong passwords, careful session handling, encryption where the platform supports it, and a clean desk for physical records all matter. And a process for a subject's request to see or delete their data is not optional; it is the duty the platform owes its users.\n\nFinally, prepare for the breach. Know who to notify, how to contain, and how to tell the users. The super admin who plans for the breach protects the users, and the trust that took years to build.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Backup, Recovery and Business Continuity',
                'summary' => 'Be ready for the day the system fails.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Every system fails eventually. Backup, recovery and business continuity is the discipline of ensuring that when it does, the platform returns quickly and loses nothing that matters.\n\nStart with the backup, and then verify it. A backup that has never been restored is a backup that probably will not work. The super admin who restores into a staging copy on a schedule knows the backup is real, and the one who only checks that it ran knows nothing at all.\n\nDefine the recovery objectives. How much data can the platform afford to lose, and how fast must it return? Those two numbers, the recovery point and the recovery time, drive every decision about how often to back up and how to design the recovery.\n\nWrite the runbook. When the system fails at the worst moment, the person on duty should not be improvising. A written runbook, with the steps, the owners, and the contacts, turns a panic into a procedure.\n\nFinally, rehearse the recovery. The teams that recover well are the teams that have rehearsed, at least once, under time pressure. The super admin who drills the recovery turns a potential disaster into an afternoon's work.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Vendor and Integrator Governance',
                'summary' => 'Manage the partners who build and run.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The platform is built and run with vendors and integrators. Vendor and integrator governance is the discipline of managing those partners so their work serves the mission and does not capture it.\n\nStart with the contract. Scope, service levels, security duties, and the exit clause belong in writing, because the partner who can walk away without a contract is a partner who holds the platform hostage. The exit clause, in particular, is what keeps a partner honest.\n\nKeep the keys to your own house. Source code, configuration, data exports, and documentation must be accessible to the platform's own team. A vendor whose work only they can operate is a vendor the platform cannot replace, and that dependency is a governance failure.\n\nReview their performance against the service levels. A partner measured on agreed service levels is a partner who delivers; a partner measured on nothing delivers what suits them. The super admin who reviews, and acts on the review, keeps the partnership healthy.\n\nFinally, plan the succession. Every critical vendor should have a documented alternative, or an internal capability to replace them. The super admin who governs vendors deliberately keeps the platform independent, and independence is what keeps the mission safe.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Building a Governance Culture',
                'summary' => 'Make good governance the way things are done.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Rules do not govern; habits do. Building a governance culture is the discipline of making good governance the way the organisation does things, rather than a checklist it endures.\n\nStart with tone at the top. The organisation follows what its leaders actually do, not what they say. A super admin who follows the access rules, who logs their own changes, and who asks for the audit rather than avoiding it sets the tone that governs everyone else.\n\nMake the right thing the easy thing. Governance fails when the compliant path is harder than the shortcut. Build the process so that the standard way is also the fast way, and the culture follows the path of least resistance, as it always does.\n\nTeach the why, not just the rule. People follow rules they understand and evade rules they do not. Every governance rule should come with its reason, because the reason is what a person remembers when the rule is inconvenient.\n\nFinally, measure the culture. Near-misses reported, audit findings fixed on time, and access reviews completed are the signs of a culture that works. The super admin who builds the culture, rather than just the rules, leaves behind a platform that governs itself.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    15 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Payment Partners and Integration',
                'summary' => 'Connect the platform to the money flow.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The platform's payments run through partners, and the integration with them decides whether money moves reliably. Payment partners and integration is the discipline of choosing, connecting, and monitoring the rails the platform depends on.\n\nStart with the partner choice. What methods do your users need, what does the partner charge, how fast is settlement, and how reliable are they? The payment partner is a business relationship as much as a technical one, and it should be chosen on all four questions, not on price alone.\n\nIntegrate for the edge cases. The happy path is easy; the real work is what happens when a payment fails, times out, or arrives twice. Idempotency, reconciliation, and clear failure handling are what make an integration production-grade rather than a demo.\n\nMonitor every transaction. A payment that fails silently is a customer lost and a support case opened. The operations team that watches for failed, pending, and stuck payments catches the problem while it can still be fixed.\n\nFinally, keep the partner honest with data. Settlement reports, failure rates, and dispute counts are the evidence of whether the partner performs. The operations lead who reviews the partner's numbers keeps the money flowing and the users trusting.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Wallet Product Features',
                'summary' => 'Design wallet features users can trust.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The wallet is where users keep their money on the platform, and its features shape their trust. Wallet product features is the discipline of designing wallet behaviour that is clear, safe, and useful.\n\nStart with the ledger. Every wallet is a ledger of entries: money in, money out, holds, and the balance. Design every feature against the ledger, because a wallet that cannot explain every entry is a wallet users will not trust.\n\nMake holds and pending explicit. A hold is money reserved but not yet settled, and the user must see it as distinct from their available balance. Confusing held money with spendable money is the most common wallet complaint, and the clearest display fixes it.\n\nDesign the payout and refund paths deliberately. How money leaves the wallet, how fast, and what it costs are product decisions, not technical ones. The user who knows the payout timeline does not open a support ticket on day two of a three-day payout.\n\nFinally, build in the safety rails: confirmation steps, limits, and alerts on unusual activity. The wallet that is easy to use and hard to abuse is the wallet that earns both volume and trust, and the operations team that designs it does both.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Escalation and Critical Incident Handling',
                'summary' => 'Run the response when money is at stake.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "When payments fail or money goes missing, the response must be fast and certain. Escalation and critical incident handling is the discipline of running that response when money is at stake and every minute counts.\n\nStart with the severity levels. Define, in advance, what makes an incident critical: money lost, a payment partner down, a suspected breach. The severity drives who is paged, how fast, and what authority they have, because a critical incident is no time to invent a hierarchy.\n\nEstablish the single incident owner. Every incident has one person accountable for the response, even when many are working on it. Without a single owner, the incident fragments into parallel guesses, and the fix arrives late.\n\nCommunicate on a rhythm. Stakeholders and affected users need updates on a schedule, even when the update is still investigating. The team that communicates on a rhythm keeps trust; the team that goes silent loses it faster than the incident itself.\n\nFinally, run the post-incident review without blame. What failed, what worked, and what changes are the outputs that make the next incident smaller. The operations lead who learns from every critical incident turns a series of crises into a steadily more reliable platform.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Analytics for Support and Payments',
                'summary' => 'Read the numbers that run the operation.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Support and payments generate numbers, and those numbers are the operation's early-warning system. Analytics for support and payments is the discipline of reading them well enough to act before the user complains.\n\nStart with the core metrics. For support: volume, first response time, resolution time, and reopen rate. For payments: success rate, failure rate, settlement time, and dispute rate. These few numbers tell you whether the operation is healthy or quietly failing.\n\nWatch the trends, not just the levels. A failure rate of two percent is fine until it was one percent yesterday. The operator who watches the trend catches the drift while it is still small; the one who watches only the level discovers it at the crisis.\n\nSegment to find the cause. Totals hide problems: a payment method failing for one region, or a support topic spiking for one feature. The analyst who segments by method, region, and topic finds the cause that the total conceals.\n\nFinally, turn every insight into an action. A metric understood but not acted on is a metric wasted. The operations team that closes the loop from number to action is the team whose support load falls and whose payments keep flowing.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Team Leadership and Rostering',
                'summary' => 'Lead the team that keeps the platform running.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Support and payments are people operations, and the team's shape decides the service. Team leadership and rostering is the discipline of building and scheduling the team so the platform is always covered and the people are not burned out.\n\nStart with the workload. How many tickets and cases arrive per hour, and how long does each take? The workload divided by the capacity is the staffing need, and the leader who staffs from the data, not from guesswork, is never caught short on a busy day.\n\nRoster for the peaks. Volume is not flat; it peaks at certain hours and days. A roster that matches the peaks keeps response times down without overstaffing the quiet hours, and the team that is rostered well does more with the same people.\n\nDevelop the people. A clear path from junior to senior, with the skills named and the coaching given, keeps good people. The leader who develops the team retains it, and a retained team knows the platform deeply enough to solve what a new team cannot.\n\nFinally, watch the humans. Burnout shows in the numbers before it shows in the people: slower responses, more errors, more absences. The leader who reads those signs and acts keeps the team, and the team is what keeps the platform running.",
            ],
        ],
    ],
];
